Validate match distances on a wrapping output buffer too.

tinfl only rejected an out-of-range match distance under
TINFL_FLAG_USING_NON_WRAPPING_OUTPUT_BUF, because the test it used --
`dist > dist_from_out_buf_start` -- is only meaningful there. On a wrapping
output buffer that offset returns to 0 every TINFL_LZ_DICT_SIZE bytes, so it
says where we are in the window rather than how much of the window was ever
written, and gating the whole check on the flag left a wrapping caller with
no distance validation at all.

mz_inflate() sets that flag only for an MZ_FINISH on the first call, so every
streaming caller was in that position. A stream claiming a distance larger
than the bytes emitted so far -- or one of the reserved distance codes 30 and
31, whose s_dist_base entry is 0 -- would then read
`pOut_buf_start + ((dist_from_out_buf_start - dist) & out_buf_size_mask)`,
wrapping into the dictionary that mz_inflateInit2() obtained from malloc()
and never cleared, and return its contents as decompressed data with no
error. RFC 1951 3.2.5 requires such a stream to be rejected.

This tracks bytes emitted since tinfl_init() in the decompressor and bounds
the distance by min(that, window size), which is what zlib does with
state->whave. miniz exposes no preset-dictionary API, so history cannot exist
by any other means and no valid stream is rejected. Written to be
upstreamable as-is.

--- a/miniz.h
+++ b/miniz.h
@@ -1041,6 +1041,11 @@
         mz_uint32 m_state, m_num_bits, m_zhdr0, m_zhdr1, m_z_adler32, m_final, m_type, m_check_adler32, m_dist, m_counter, m_num_extra, m_table_sizes[TINFL_MAX_HUFF_TABLES];
         tinfl_bit_buf_t m_bit_buf;
         size_t m_dist_from_out_buf_start;
+        /* Total bytes emitted since tinfl_init(), saturating is not needed:
+           it only ever feeds a MZ_MIN against the LZ window size. Used to
+           bound match distances on a wrapping output buffer, where the
+           offset into that buffer cannot say how much history is real. */
+        size_t m_total_out;
         mz_int16 m_look_up[TINFL_MAX_HUFF_TABLES][TINFL_FAST_LOOKUP_SIZE];
         mz_int16 m_tree_0[TINFL_MAX_HUFF_SYMBOLS_0 * 2];
         mz_int16 m_tree_1[TINFL_MAX_HUFF_SYMBOLS_1 * 2];
--- a/miniz.c
+++ b/miniz.c
@@ -2478,6 +2478,7 @@
 
         bit_buf = num_bits = dist = counter = num_extra = r->m_zhdr0 = r->m_zhdr1 = 0;
         r->m_z_adler32 = r->m_check_adler32 = 1;
+        r->m_total_out = 0;
         if (decomp_flags & TINFL_FLAG_PARSE_ZLIB_HEADER)
         {
             TINFL_GET_BYTE(1, r->m_zhdr0);
@@ -2784,9 +2785,31 @@
                     }
 
                     dist_from_out_buf_start = pOut_buf_cur - pOut_buf_start;
-                    if ((dist == 0 || dist > dist_from_out_buf_start || dist_from_out_buf_start == 0) && (decomp_flags & TINFL_FLAG_USING_NON_WRAPPING_OUTPUT_BUF))
                     {
-                        TINFL_CR_RETURN_FOREVER(37, TINFL_STATUS_FAILED);
+                        /* How much history a match may legally reach back
+                           into: every byte emitted since tinfl_init(),
+                           capped by the LZ window. RFC 1951 3.2.5 forbids a
+                           distance beyond that, and with no preset
+                           dictionary API there is no other way for history
+                           to exist, so this rejects no valid stream.
